questions.explanation:
This question validates the ability to compose modules with environment-driven data while avoiding duplication and maintaining clean state separation. It covers essential skills including Terraform's external data source and locals for environment-specific configuration, for_each to create per-environment resources from a map, wrapper modules for consistent tagging (Environment, CostCenter), per-environment backend configuration for isolated state, and CI gates with fmt/validate/tflint.

## Code Example
```terraform
# ip_allocations.json
{
  "dev": "10.0.0.0/16",
  "stage": "10.1.0.0/16",
  "prod": "10.2.0.0/16"
}
```

```terraform
# main.tf (external data source + locals + subnet module usage)
data "external" "ip_allocations" {
  program = ["python3", "${path.module}/read_ip_allocations.py"]
}

locals {
  env_cidr = jsondecode(data.external.ip_allocations.result)
}

module "subnets" {
  source   = "./modules/subnets"
  for_each = toset(["dev", "stage", "prod"])

  vpc_id     = module.vpc.vpc_id
  cidr_block = local.env_cidr[each.key]
  environment = each.key
}
```

```terraform
# wrapper module (tags)
# modules/vpc-wrapper/main.tf
locals {
  default_tags = {
    Environment = var.environment
    CostCenter  = var.cost_center
  }
}

module "vpc" {
  source = "../shared-vpc"

  tags = merge(local.default_tags, var.extra_tags)
}
```

```terraform
# backend.tf (per-environment)
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    key = "${var.environment}/terraform.tfstate"
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This tests cross-cloud module design, provider aliasing, and policy-driven governance at scale.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform multi-provider modules
- Provider aliasing and per-tenant scoping
- Open Policy Agent integration with terraform show -json plan
- CI gates and fail-fast

## Code Example
```javascript
// Module interface skeleton (Terraform-like)
module data_lake {
  source = ./modules/data_lake
  tenants = {
    t1 = { cloud = aws, region = us-east-1 }
    t2 = { cloud = gcp, region = us-central1 }
  }
  quotas = {
    t1 = { data_lake_size_gb = 100 }
    t2 = { data_lake_size_gb = 200 }
  }
}
```

```javascript
// Sample OPA policy skeleton
package terraform.policy
default allow = false
allow {
  input.resource_changes[_].change.actions[_] == create
}
```

